\section{What is conditioned on}{

The density is CONDITIONAL on the draw's own group-level values:
\code{frm_sample()} samples \code{b} alongside everything else, so each row of
the draws matrix is a complete parameter vector and no integration is
left to do. This is exactly brms's convention, where the Stan model
also samples the group-level parameters. The consequence is worth
stating plainly: \code{loo()} on such a matrix is leave-one-OBSERVATION-out
with the groups held fixed, not leave-one-group-out, and for a model
with few observations per group the two differ.

Addition terms enter exactly as they enter the fitted objective,
because they enter through the same code: \code{cens()} replaces a row's
density by the matching CDF difference, \code{trunc()} divides by the
window mass, \code{weights()} multiplies the row's contribution, and
\code{trials()} is the family's own argument. brms composes them in the
same order (\code{log_lik_censor()}, \code{log_lik_truncate()},
\code{log_lik_weight()}).
}

\section{Multivariate models}{

With \code{set_rescor(TRUE)} a column is the joint density of the row's
response VECTOR, so the matrix keeps one column per observation.
Without \code{rescor}, the responses are independent given the predictors
and the default sums their log-densities per row; pass \code{resp} to get
one response's contribution alone.
}

\section{Likelihoods with no per-observation column}{

A model whose smallest independent unit is a group has no
per-observation column to leave out, and this refuses rather than
inventing one: R-side residual correlation (\link{frmtmb-autocor}), a
\code{\link[=hmm]{hmm()}} sequence, and a group-level mixture (\code{mixture(groups = )}).
An \code{\link[=lca]{lca()}} subject is one row, so its column is well defined and is
not refused. In-model imputation (\code{mi()}, \code{me()}) is refused for the
same kind of reason: a latent value is a parameter, not an
observation. Use \code{AIC()} on the maximum-likelihood fits or
\code{\link[=frm_bootstrap]{frm_bootstrap()}} for those.
}

